  "query_preview": "..\n\nMuch above normal temperatures today as highs reach into the lower \n70s behind a warm front lifting northward through Michigan. Normal \nhighs for the end of October are in the upper 50s so we'll be 10-15 \ndegrees above normal. The front is running up against a very dry \nairmass with the persistent surface ridging that has been over the \nregion for several days now so will pass with little in the way of \nshowers. There were a few overnight over SW lower aided by a \neastward tracking mid level wave, but were not able to drift much \neastward into SE MI. Surface ridge will be sliding southeast through \nthe day keeping us on the western side of it with increasingly \nsouthwesterly flow. This added warm air advection will help \nstabilize the airmass and provide the added boost in temperatures \nover the mid to upper 60s we've been experiencing. \n\nBest chance of rain in a couple weeks comes Thursday as a low coming \ntogether over the Canadian Plains tracks east across Ontario within \nan increasingly more zonal mid level flow pattern. Models have been \npretty consistent with this system, showing a strong low level jet \nflaring up across IA and WI tonight with the stacked mid level wave \nand surface low over western Lake Superior produce a good coverage \nof showers and thunderstorms tonight. The forcing will get pulled \nnorth of the area as the system progresses east so by the time the \ncold front makes it to SE MI we'll have the decreasing LLJ at around \n35 knots with a narrow corridor of theta e and fgen. Mid level lapse \nrates are not overly impressive in the 6 C/km range with most models \noffering no CAPE. PWATs do jump up to around an inch with the ribbon \nof moisture ahead of the front so could still see some showers. \nWe're continuing with a chance of showers Thursday across the area \nwith mainly a 30-40 pop resulting in less than a tenth of QPF.  \n\nHigh pressure builds in from Canada behind the front bringing cooler \ntemps (850mb around 7-8C) and dry conditions Friday. Highs Friday \nwill be in the low 60s with lows both Thursday and Friday nights \ndipping into the upper 30s for many locations. As the ridge tried to \nbecome re-established across the Plains heading into the weekend, \nthere is remnants of a southern stream wave drifting into the Ohio \nValley which will push a warm front back near the state line. GFS is \nmuch more bullish on the strength of this feature that the ECMWF, so \nat this point will expect increasing clouds with the front nearby \nand possibility of some showers Saturday near the state line. \nOverall the ridging will keep the region mild with above normal max \ntemps in the 60s most of the forecast.",
